Why would you need to merge or join data?
	Rarely will you have a request that is isolated in one location. Moreover, one data source typically doesn’t have all the data points needed for a thorough analysis. Much of this can be explained by how relational databases are structured. Each table within a database has the sole purpose to reduce redundancy.
	When I talk about redundancy, let’s put this into perspective. Instead of carrying the “space” for employee data with a transactional data table, you would separate them and merge them together when necessary. This way, you save space on your server because you don’t necessarily need to have everything that occurred for every transaction. This could be a few transactions in a day to several billion when you consider the stock market!
	Furthermore, merging and joining data is a fundamental requirement for creating a comprehensive dataset that can support complex business intelligence and machine learning tasks (McKinney, 2022). In a sense, you are reconstructing these relationships that were separated during the data collection or storage process for the reason I mentioned above. Without the ability to merge these disparate sources, the data remains siloed, and the analyst is limited to looking at isolated variables rather than the “big picture”.
	This also allows for cross-validation because you wouldn’t be able to join tables if 2 relational tables do not match. Of course, you have different methods of joining (i.e., left, right, inner, outer) that can highlight some of these records/discrepancies. It is also important to note that not all tables are built to be joined/merged together. There is typically a “primary key” that assists with joining specific tables together which may result into you requiring a third or forth table to achieve the desired relationship.
